export const REPO_GUIDE_DOCS: readonly RepoGuideDoc[] = [{"path":"templates/policy-repo/README.md","markdown":"# Your policy repository\n\nThis repository holds the rules ZIFFER enforces for your agents, and your\nsignature over them. Nothing here runs on our machines. We check a signature; we\ncannot make one.\n\n**Ten minutes from here to a signed, published policy.** Six steps, in order.\nThe whole of your first hour, including the parts that happen outside this\nrepository, is `docs/onboarding/start-here.md` in the pack we sent you.\n\nIf you work with a coding agent, `npx @ziffer-io/mcp` serves this page to it\n(`get_policy_repo_guide`), checks a clone of it against the template we shipped\n(`check_policy_repo`), and reads a failed publish run back to you\n(`explain_publish_failure`).\n\n---\n\n## What is in here\n\n```\npolicy/                      the bundle. Everything in it is covered by one signature.\n  manifest.json              who wrote this, who reviewed it, which tenant, when it expires\n  attesters/registry.json    who may approve, and their public keys        <- from us\n  door_identities.json       the service that shows decisions to your people <- from us\n  receipt_identity.json      the key that signs your receipts              <- from us\n  floors.json                how sensitive each resource is\n  risk_functions.json        how an action's risk is computed\n  reversibility.json         which actions can be undone\n  notice_targets.json        who is told when an irreversible action runs\n  alert_targets.json         who is paged when something is refused\n  adapters.json              your front doors, and the fidelity each one stamps\n  limits.json                the three windows this deployment runs on\n  examples/                  one proposal per action you gate. The pull request\n                             workflow grades every one of them against your rules.\nbin/new-signing-key.sh       one command: your signing key and its public half\n.github/workflows/           validate and scan on a pull request; sign and publish on merge\n```\n\n**Three of those files come from us and you sign them with the rest.** They are\nmarked above. Overwrite all three with the files on your onboarding sheet before\nyour first publish. The copies in this template belong to a demonstration tenant\ncalled `ten-example`; a bundle that still carries them is refused by name when\nyou publish (`BundleProvisionedMemberMismatch`), which is the right answer and a\nwasted afternoon.\n\n**Two of them are one pair.** `door_identities.json` names the service that shows\nyour approvers what they are authorising and signs what they were shown;\n`attesters/registry.json` says who your approvers are. We produce both together,\nbecause an approval given at a service your policy does not name is refused after\nthe person has been told it worked. Ask us for both when either changes.\n\n**There is no `SIGNATURE` file here, on purpose.** A signature is made by your\nkey over your bytes, in the publish job. A template carrying somebody else's\nsignature would be a file that looks checked and verifies nothing.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Take a copy\n\nUse this repository as a template, or copy the tree. It becomes yours: private,\nin your organisation, under your review rules. Policy changes are pull requests\nfrom here on.\n\n## 2. Make your signing key\n\n```\nbin/new-signing-key.sh\n```\n\nIt writes the private key to `~/.ziffer/policy-signing.key` and the public half\nto `policy-signing.pub.json` in this repository. **Commit the public half.\nNever commit the private one.** We never see it, we cannot recover it, and\nnothing we run can sign your policy with it. That is the point of the whole\narrangement.\n\n## 3. Add five secrets and eight variables\n\nIn **Settings -> Secrets and variables -> Actions**:\n\n| | Name | What it is |\n|---|---|---|\n| Secret | `POLICY_SIGNING_KEY` | the text of the private key step 2 made |\n| Secret | `ZIFFER_CLIENT_CERT` | the CI certificate we signed for you |\n| Secret | `ZIFFER_CLIENT_KEY` | its private key, which you made and we never saw |\n| Secret | `ZIFFER_CA` | the authority your runner checks us against |\n| Secret | `ZIFFER_SECRETS_TOKEN` | a fine-grained token you create, allowed to write this repository's secrets and nothing else, so the renewal job can store the renewed certificate |\n| Variable | `ZIFFER_PUBLISH_URL` | where your signed policy goes |\n| Variable | `ZIFFER_CLI_URL` | where the `ziffer` tool is downloaded from |\n| Variable | `ZIFFER_CLI_VERSION` | which version |\n| Variable | `ZIFFER_CLI_SHA256` | that artifact's checksum, so a substituted binary never runs |\n| Variable | `ZIFFER_RENEW_VERSION` | the renewal client's own version, which is not the `ziffer` tool's |\n| Variable | `ZIFFER_RENEW_SHA256` | the renewal client's checksum |\n| Variable | `ZIFFER_ISSUER_ADDR` | where the renewal job asks for a new certificate |\n| Variable | `ZIFFER_ISSUER_NAME` | the name that address's certificate must carry |\n\n`install.md` section 5 says where each one comes from. The eight variables are not\nsecrets and are deliberately not baked into the workflow: a download location\nwritten into a handout is the one that stops moving when the real one does.\n\nThe publish job also needs an **environment** called `ziffer-production`\n(Settings -> Environments). It is the second lock on the signing key,\nindependent of branch protection: a job with no environment reads the key on any\npush that reaches it. Branch protection is the first lock, and it is\n\"Lock the branch\" below.\n\n## 4. Put your own rules in\n\nThree files decide what happens to an action:\n\n* `floors.json` names each resource your agents touch and how sensitive it is.\n  **A resource you do not list is treated as the most sensitive one there is.**\n* `risk_functions.json` gives each action a base risk and the conditions that\n  raise it.\n* `reversibility.json` says which actions can be undone. **An action you do not\n  list is treated as irreversible.**\n\nBoth of those defaults are deliberate and they are the ones you want: the\nunknown case is the one you have not thought about yet.\n\n`docs/onboarding/policy-by-example.md` walks every file line by line. Put one\nproposal per gated action in `policy/examples/` while you are there: the\npull request workflow grades each of them against the rules in the pull\nrequest, so a change that would start refusing your agent's ordinary work shows\nup in review instead of at three in the morning.\n\n## 5. Open a pull request\n\nThe `policy-validate` workflow runs with no key at all. It prints exactly what\nyour signature would cover, then grades every example against the proposed\nrules. It cannot publish anything.\n\nThe `policy-scan` workflow runs `npx -y @ziffer-io/scan@0 --ci \"$POLICY_DIR\"`\nover your policy folder (`POLICY_DIR`, `policy/` as shipped), also with no key. It starts the MCP servers this repository configures for your AI\nagents (`.mcp.json`, `.cursor/mcp.json`, `.vscode/mcp.json` and the like), asks\neach one only for its list of tools, and prints what the proposed rules decide\nfor every tool, one line each. A tool with no risk function is refused at\n`8.4-3`: the job fails and prints the line to add to `risk_functions.json`, a\ndraft to review before you merge it. A repository that configures no MCP server\ngets one `NOT CHECKED` line and a green job.\n\n## 6. Merge\n\nThe `publish-policy` workflow reads the epoch we hold, writes the next one into\nyour manifest, checks that the committed public key is your signing key's,\nsigns, verifies what it signed, and publishes over a mutually authenticated\nconnection. When it prints `active`, every process that reads policy is serving\nyour new rules. That is seconds, not an operator's working hours.\n\nIf it refuses, it prints the refusal's name and what to do about it. The full\ntable is `docs/onboarding/policy-ci.md` section 5.\n\n---\n\n<!-- guide:branch-protection -->\n## Lock the branch\n\n**Nothing above is change control until the branch is protected.** The publish\njob signs whatever lands on `main`, so a direct push to `main` is a policy\nchange with no review in front of it, made with your signing key. The\n`ziffer-production` environment is the second lock and it guards the key; this\nis the first one and it guards the rules.\n\nIn **Settings -> Branches -> Add branch protection rule**, for `main`:\n\n| Setting | Set it to |\n|---|---|\n| **Require a pull request before merging** | on |\n| **Require approvals** | at least 1 — `author.id` and `reviewer.id` have to be different people, and this is where the second one comes from |\n| **Dismiss stale pull request approvals when new commits are pushed** | on, so an approval covers the bytes that merge and not an earlier draft |\n| **Require status checks to pass before merging** | on, with `policy-validate` selected — it is the job that grades your examples against the proposed rules |\n| **Do not allow bypassing the above settings** | on, or an administrator can push straight to `main` and the rest of this table is advisory |\n\nConfirm it after your first publish, not before: the status check appears in\nthat list only once the workflow has run at least once.\n\nWe cannot check this for you and nothing we run can see it. It is your\nrepository, and the setting lives in your organisation's account.\n<!-- guide:/branch-protection -->\n\n---\n\n## Adding a person who can approve\n\nAn approver is a person with a passkey or a hardware key, enrolled once, and\nthen named in this repository under your signature. We send you their entry as a\npatch to `policy/attesters/registry.json`. You apply it, review it like any\nother change, and merge:\n\n```\ngit apply approver-<name>.patch\n```\n\nThe patch adds two things that have to agree: the entry itself, a block of\nseveral lines under the person's name, and that person's assurance level beside\nit, one line. It changes nothing else. Read\n`docs/onboarding/approvers.md` for what an approver sees and what happens when\none is away.\n\n**We cannot add an approver to your policy.** We can only hand you the patch.\nAnyone who could add themselves to your registry could approve their own\nactions, so nobody can, including us.\n\n---\n\n## The two things worth knowing before you plan around them\n\n**The epoch only rises.** Every publish must carry a higher `bundle_epoch` than\nthe last one we accepted, and the workflow now reads that number from us rather\nthan asking you to type it. There is no rollback: a rule change that turns out\nwrong is fixed by publishing again, which takes about as long as this pipeline.\n\n**`author.id` and `reviewer.id` must be different people.** The signing tool\nrefuses before it signs. Two-person review of policy is a rule here, not a\nconvention.\n"},{"path":"docs/onboarding/start-here.md","markdown":"# Start here — your first hour\n\nYou have a sheet from us with an account name, three policy files, a receipt\nverification key and an API key. This page is the shortest path from that sheet\nto a verified receipt for an action a person approved. Six steps, about an hour,\nthe last twenty minutes of it watching it work.\n\n**Nothing on this page is new.** Every command is quoted from the guide that\nexplains it, and each block says which one, so when you want the\nreasoning it is one click away and there is only ever one description of each\nthing.\n\n**What you need in front of you:** your onboarding sheet, a terminal, a browser,\nand somewhere to put a private repository.\n\n| | Step | Roughly |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Install the tool | 5 min |\n| 2 | Your policy repository and your signing key | 10 min |\n| 3 | Enrol yourself as the person who approves | 5 min |\n| 4 | Publish your first policy | 10 min |\n| 5 | Three lines in your AI agent | 15 min |\n| 6 | Watch a low, a high and a forbidden action | 10 min |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Install the tool\n\nOne command. It checks the prerequisites, verifies the release signature before\nit downloads a binary, installs `ziffer`, makes your signing key and writes the\ntwo certificate requests.\n\n> Quoted from [install.md § 0.1](install.md).\n\n```bash\ncurl -fsSL https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/releases/latest/download/install.sh | sh\n```\n\nPress Enter at every prompt for a complete install. `--dry-run` shows every\ncommand without running one; `--yes` asks nothing. If it refuses, it refuses by\nname and does nothing after it — the names are in the same section.\n\n**Send us back** the two certificate requests it wrote and your public signing\nkey. **Keep** the private key. We never see it, and nothing we run can sign your\npolicy with it.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Your policy repository and your signing key\n\nTake a copy of the policy repository template we sent you. It is the complete\nexample policy, its three pipeline files ready to run, and one command for the key.\n\n> Quoted from the template's `README.md`.\n\n```bash\nbin/new-signing-key.sh\n```\n\nIt writes the private key outside the repository and the public half inside it.\nCommit the public half; put the private key's text into the repository secret\n`POLICY_SIGNING_KEY`. Then the four other secrets and the eight variables, and\none environment called `ziffer-production` — the README has the table, and\n`install.md` section 5 says where each value comes from.\n\n**Overwrite the three files marked \"from us\"** with the ones on your sheet:\n`receipt_identity.json`, `door_identities.json` and `attesters/registry.json`.\nA bundle still carrying the demonstration tenant's copies is refused by name.\n\n`door_identities.json` names the service that shows your approvers what they are\nauthorising and signs what they were shown. For you that is the approval service\nat the link in your invitation — the page in section 3 below — and it is not a\nsetting: it follows from who is in your registry. Change one and the other has to\nchange with it, so we produce both together and your publish is refused if they\ndisagree.\n\n**`attesters/registry.json` arrives empty, and section 3 is what fills it.** Until\nsomebody is in it, nothing that needs an approval can get one: an action that asks\nfor one waits and then expires. That is why enrolling yourself comes before your\nfirst publish and not after it.\n\nThen make the rules yours. `floors.json`, `risk_functions.json` and\n`reversibility.json` are the three that decide what happens to an action, and\n[policy-by-example.md](policy-by-example.md) walks every file line by line.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Enrol yourself as the person who approves\n\nYour administrator creates an invitation link with `invite-approver.sh`, which\nthey download and verify from the release ([install.md § 11](install.md)), and\nthe admin certificate we signed for them ([approvers.md § 2](approvers.md)). It\nconnects over the internet to the address on the `ZIFFER_APPROVAL_ADDR` row of\nyour handover sheet. They send the link to you. Open it on\nthe device holding the passkey or hardware key you want to use, press\n**Register this device**, and unlock it. The page shows the line of policy your\nkey produced.\n\n> Quoted from [approvers.md § 3](approvers.md).\n\n```bash\ngit apply approver-jane_o.patch\n```\n\nThat is us handing you the entry as a patch to `policy/attesters/registry.json`,\nand you applying it. It adds two things that have to agree — the entry, and your\nassurance level beside it — and changes nothing else. Review it like any other\nchange.\n\n**We cannot add an approver to your policy.** Anybody who could would be able to\napprove their own actions.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Publish your first policy\n\n**Your first signed policy reaches us at enrolment**: you hand us the signed\nfolder out of band, and we verify it under your public key before anything is\nplaced; a folder that does not verify is refused and nothing is placed. **Every\npolicy after it, your own CI publishes** (`policy-ci.md`), and this step is the\nfirst of those.\n\nOpen a pull request. The `policy-validate` workflow runs with no key at all: it\nprints exactly what your signature would cover, then grades every proposal in\n`policy/examples/` against the rules in the pull request. You can run the same\ngrading on your own machine before you push:\n\n> Quoted from [policy-ci.md § 4](policy-ci.md).\n\n```bash\nziffer decide  <bundle-dir> --unsigned --now <RFC3339> --proposal <file>\n```\n\nMerge it. The `publish-policy` workflow reads the epoch we hold, writes the next\none into your manifest, checks that the committed public key is your signing\nkey's, signs, verifies what it signed, and publishes.\n\n**When it prints `active`, your rules are in force** — every process that reads\npolicy is serving them. That is seconds, not an operator's working hours. If it\nrefuses, it prints the refusal's name and what to do; the table is\n[policy-ci.md § 5](policy-ci.md).\n\n---\n\n## 5. Three lines in your AI agent\n\nPropose, wait, verify. Your action runs only after the receipt holds.\n\n> Quoted from [sdk.md § 8](sdk.md).\n\n```python\n    decision = client.wait_for_receipt(client.propose(proposal).decision_id, timeout=30.0)\n    if decision.receipt is None:\n        raise PermissionError(f\"ziffer refused: {decision.refusal_category}\")\n```\n\nand, before the action happens:\n\n> Quoted from [sdk.md § 8](sdk.md).\n\n```python\n        verify(decision.receipt, json.dumps(proposal).encode(), anchor)\n```\n\nFour environment variables, each a row on your sheet:\n`ZIFFER_API_KEY` (the key **is** your tenant — you never put a tenant name in a\nrequest), `ZIFFER_API_URL`, `ZIFFER_TRUST_ANCHOR` pointing at the receipt\nverification key on your sheet, and `ZIFFER_SUITE_FLOOR`, the weakest signature\nsuite you accept. TypeScript is [sdk.md § 9](sdk.md); the whole\nexample, with what each refusal means, is the same page.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Watch a low, a high and a forbidden action\n\nSend three proposals from your AI agent, in this order, and watch what each one\ndoes. This is the whole product in ten minutes.\n\n1. **A low-risk action.** It passes. The answer carries a receipt, `verify`\n   accepts it, your action runs. Nobody was asked anything.\n2. **A high-risk action.** The answer is `ATTEST` and there is no receipt yet:\n   the decision reached the attestation stage and approvers are being asked.\n   Your phone lights up. The page opens **locked** — it carries nothing about\n   the action until your key opens it — and once you approve, the action\n   releases and the receipt appears on your next read.\n3. **A forbidden action.** It is refused as `PolicyRefused`; the clause that\n   refused it is in the console and your audit chain, not in the answer. Do\n   not retry it: the same bytes get the same answer, and that is the point.\n\n`ATTEST` is the gate, not a refusal. Do not re-propose on it — the proposal is\nalready in flight, and a second one is a second action.\n[sdk.md § 5](sdk.md) is what every refusal means; [sdk.md § 6](sdk.md) is how to\nlist everything we are holding for you.\n\n---\n\n## When something does not work\n\nEvery refusal in this system has a name, and the name is the answer. Quote it\nback to us rather than describing the symptom: `support.md` has what we need\nwith it and what we can and cannot see from our side.\n\n| Where it went wrong | Read |\n|---|---|\n| the install | [install.md](install.md) — the refusal table in § 0.1 |\n| the pipeline | [policy-ci.md § 5](policy-ci.md) — every answer the publish endpoint gives |\n| the rules themselves | [policy-by-example.md](policy-by-example.md) |\n| approvals | [approvers.md](approvers.md) |\n| the SDK | [sdk.md § 5](sdk.md) |\n| anything else | [support.md](support.md) |\n\n## What this hour does not cover\n\nThe default rung works end to end, and it is the one this hour sets up. To run the\nExecutor in your own environment — the second rung, where the credential that\ntouches your systems never leaves your network — write to hello@ziffer.io.\n"}];
